  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Satish Gangwal vs Ram Singh ,Food Inspector on 15 March, 2016 1 M.Cr.C.No.8502/2013 Satish Gangwal Vs. Ramsingh & Others 15.03.2016 Shri R.K. Sharma, counsel for the applicant.",
    "facts": "It was informed by the applicant that the aforesaid chili powder was purchased by him from one Bhagwan Singh who was proprietor of \"Kushwah Masala Udyog, Vidisha\". In appeal, the applicant has moved an application under Section 391 of Cr.P.C. that the respondents Bhagwan Singh and Brijmohan Kushwaha are real brother and they got a certificate of agmark for various items of Mahashakti Brand spices and he gave description of brothers of Bhagwan Singh who were partner of \"Kushwah Masala Udyog\" and according to Section 20-A of the PF Act, actual manufacturer of chili powder should have been punished and therefore, it was prayed that the certificate issued relating to agmark authorization dated 24-01-2011 be taken on record. The State in its reply to the application has accepted the application, however the appellate Court vide order dated 20- 09-2013 has dismissed the application.",
    "arguments": "After considering the submissions made by learned counsel for the parties and looking to the facts and circumstances of the case, it appears that the applicant relies upon the judgment of the Apex Court in the case of \"Rambhau and another Vs. State of Maharashtra\", {2001 SCC (Cri.) 812} in which condition has been laid by the Apex Court that when additional evidence can be taken at the appellate stage. Learned counsel for the applicant also invited the attention of this Court to the judgment passed by the Apex Court in the case of \"Zahira Habibulla H. Sheikh and another Vs. State of Gujarat and others\" {2004 SCC (Cri.) 999} which is related to Articles 21 and 19 of the Constitution of India.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "After considering the submissions made by learned counsel for the parties and looking to the facts and circumstances of the case, it appears that the applicant relies upon the judgment of the Apex Court in the case of \"Rambhau and another Vs. State of Maharashtra\", {2001 SCC (Cri.) 812} in which condition has been laid by the Apex Court that when additional evidence can be taken at the appellate stage. Learned counsel for the applicant also invited the attention of this Court to the judgment passed by the Apex Court in the case of \"Zahira Habibulla H. Sheikh and another Vs. State of Gujarat and others\" {2004 SCC (Cri.) 999} which is related to Articles 21 and 19 of the Constitution of India.",
